**The cited finding was stale, but the defect class was live and worse than described.** The five `.unwrap()`s the phase names were already converted to `map_err` in commit `84b1c44` (phase-025), and the sites live in `src/runtime.rs`, not `interpreter.rs:924` (that line is `fn push_scope`). So `grep -rn 'unwrap()' src/` was already clean — 7 hits, all in `mod tests`.

Reproducing instead found a genuine reachable panic in the same function, needing no clock manipulation:

```
$ ./target/debug/rb run  # say time.format(1e300, "%Y")
panicked at src/runtime.rs:510: overflow when adding duration to `SystemTime`
Error: RuntimeError: The interpreter thread stopped unexpectedly
```

`timestamp as u64` saturates, so `1e300` became `u64::MAX` and the addition panicked from inside `std`. A Redblue `try`/`catch error` could not catch it. The same cast made `time.format(-1)` print `1970-01-01` instead of `1969-12-31`.

Fixed by extracting `since_epoch(instant, span)` — the instant is a parameter, so a test passes a pre-epoch instant without touching the machine clock — and `seconds_for_format(timestamp, span)`, which refuses a timestamp that names no date. Both clock functions sit in `src/runtime.rs:319` and `:339`.

Gates: `cargo fmt --check` pass, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` pass with no `allow(` added, `cargo test --all-targets` 1075 passed / 0 failed (39 in `stdlib_modules_test.rs`, including `network_timeouts_are_published_and_bounded`), `rb test` 340/340, all `examples/*.rb` clean. 12 new tests, 5 named `edge_*`, 4 asserting a failure.

One caveat I will not paper over: **`./rbops/verify.sh` is not in this checkout**, so I ran its four documented constituents individually rather than reporting a result I did not observe. That row in REPORT.md says exactly this.
